  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Drier cooler weather remains through the middle \nof the week, with next rain chances returning Thursday.\n\nCool, slightly below-average temperatures will linger through \nTuesday as high pressure and an upper-level trough hold firm \nover the eastern region. By Wednesday, that trough will finally \nshift northeast, allowing heights to rise while the quasi-\nstationary Bermuda high slides eastward. Warm, humid air from \nthe western Atlantic will surge back in under the influence of \nthe Bermuda high, instantly reviving mid-week summer mugginess. \nLate Wednesday into Thursday, an upper-level impulse will sweep \nsoutheast across the northern Great Lakes, dragging a surface \ncold front toward the area. This front will bring a return of \nrain and thunderstorm chances by Thursday afternoon and evening,\nthough the exact timing of its arrival will ultimately \ndetermine any threat of severe weather or flash flooding.",
